// 017 — Floating-point variables
// AiLang is an Integer-first language. Arithmetic primitives (Add,
// Multiply, Divide, Modulo) operate on 64-bit signed integers; the
// PrintNumber primitive prints integers. Float support — FPU
// dispatch, Float_Sin/Cos/Log/Pow/Exp intrinsics — is scaffolded but
// not yet surfaced as first-class Float variables.
//
// Two idioms cover most "why do I need floats?" cases in the meantime:
//
//   1. Fixed-point arithmetic. Pick a scale (e.g. ×1000 for 3 decimal
//      places), do the math in Integer, divide back when printing.
//      See 021_constants.ailang for PI×1000.
//
//   2. Rational representation. Track numerator and denominator as
//      two Integers; reduce with GCD (see 436).
//
// When Float vars land, this demo gets rewritten. For now it shows
// the fixed-point workaround on a worked example.
SubRoutine.Main {
    // "3.14159 * 2" at 5-decimal fixed-point: 314159 * 2 = 628318.
    pi_x100000 = 314159
    two_pi = Multiply(pi_x100000, 2)

    PrintMessage("2*pi ~ ")
    PrintNumber(two_pi)
    PrintMessage(" (scaled x100000 -> 6.28318)\n")
}
RunTask(Main)
